/// \defgroup grp_w3_fx_particle_modules FXParticleSystem templates, module data and saving
/// \ingroup grp_gameclient
/// \brief The particle-system template, the data parts of several
///        FXParticleSystem modules, and the helper that saves a random
///        range.
///
/// BFME 2 defines particle effects in FXParticleSystem INI blocks. Each
/// block becomes an FXParticleSystem::ParticleSystemTemplate, created or
/// reset by INI::parseParticleSystemDefinition, and its System sub-block
/// fills the template's own settings through
/// FXParticleSystem::ParticleSystemTemplate::parse. The rest of an effect is
/// made of modules: emission volumes and velocities, update, draw, life
/// event and similar parts. This group documents the template's
/// construction, copying and destruction, the data parts of five module
/// kinds, the velocity function of the outward emission velocity module,
/// and xferRandomVariable, which module data uses to save its random
/// ranges. The whole FXParticleSystem family is BFME-specific: Zero Hour
/// keeps all of a particle system's settings in one class, Zero Hour's
/// ParticleSystemInfo, and handles each emission volume and velocity type
/// in switch statements. \ref sub_gameclient describes the subsystem.
///
/// \par How a module is put together
/// The five module kinds whose info classes are documented here
/// (render-object draw, render-object update, lightning draw, life event
/// and GPU draw) are each split into three classes, named after the
/// module:
/// - an info class, such as FXParticleSystem::RenderObjectDrawModuleInfo,
///   that holds the values the module's INI fields set and saves them
///   through the Snapshot interface (its transfer function);
/// - a template class, such as RenderObjectDrawModuleTemplate, that combines
///   a category base with the info. Its parse function applies the module's
///   field table, and its special member functions call the info's (for
///   example RenderObjectDrawModuleTemplate's constructor, assignment and
///   destructor, and GpuDrawModuleTemplate's copy constructor);
/// - a runtime module created from the template, which works on particles.
///
/// The ortho, cylindrical and outward emission velocity modules save their
/// data differently: the runtime module holds its own copy of the velocity
/// ranges, and the module's own transfer function saves them. The outward
/// module's FXParticleSystem::OutwardEmissionVelocityModule::getVelocity is
/// documented here.
///
/// The field tables measure each field from the start of the template, and
/// the info part follows the template's base classes. That is how the INI
/// field names below are matched to the info members; the match is an
/// inference from the tables, the template constructors and the
/// default values, not a name stored in the info itself.
///
/// \par Saving
/// Each info transfer function documented here first transfers a version
/// record with
/// Xfer::Version1 (current and minimum version 1), then its fields in a fixed
/// order that need not follow the INI order. Random ranges go through
/// xferRandomVariable, strings and numbers through the Xfer operators, and
/// enumerations through Xfer::XferEnum with a type label. The life-event
/// and render-object update infos return at once when the Xfer is in
/// light-CRC mode (Xfer::IsLightCRC); the render-object draw, lightning
/// draw and GPU draw infos do not check it. Each of the five infos is
/// transferred by the transfer function of a class that embeds it, and
/// that function writes its own version record first.
/// \ref sub_save_load_crc explains Snapshot and Xfer.
///
/// \par Modding
/// The field names come from the retail field tables of each module
/// template. A random range is a GameClientRandomVariable (a low value, a
/// high value and a distribution), read by
/// INI::parseGameClientRandomVariable.
/// - Render-object draw: MultiRenderObjects; three groups of RenderGroupN
///   (a name), NumObjectsN, PercentN and ShaderN (a particle shader name,
///   default W3D_DIFFUSE) for N from 1 to 3; SinkOnTerrainCollision and
///   SinkRate.
/// - Render-object update: StartSizeX, StartSizeY, StartSizeZ, SizeRateX,
///   SizeRateY, SizeRateZ, SizeDampingX, SizeDampingY, SizeDampingZ, AngleZ,
///   AngularRateZ and AngularDamping, all random ranges defaulting to zero,
///   and Rotation, one of NONE, ROTATION_OFF, ROTATE_X, ROTATE_Y, ROTATE_Z
///   and ROTATE_V (default ROTATION_OFF).
/// - Lightning draw: OffsetX, OffsetY and OffsetZ (random ranges),
///   MultiChance and TileTexture.
/// - Life event: EventTime (a random range) and EventFX (an FX list name),
///   plus PerParticle and KillAfterEvent, which the template keeps in its
///   event base rather than in the life-event info.
/// - GPU draw: FramesPerRow and TotalFrames (both default 1), DetailTexture
///   and SpeedMultiplier (default 1.0).
/// - Outward emission velocity: Speed and OtherSpeed, both random ranges.
///
/// Sampling a random range uses GameClientRandomVariable::getValue, which,
/// as in Zero Hour, implements only CONSTANT and UNIFORM: a CONSTANT range
/// whose low and high differ is sampled as UNIFORM, and any other
/// distribution yields 0. It draws from the game-client random generator,
/// not the logic one (\ref page_core_frameworks_random), so particle
/// variation is not part of the lockstep simulation.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The info transfer functions define the save format of particle module
///   data. Each writes version 1 with minimum version 1, so a change to a
///   field list or its order breaks older saves unless the version is raised
///   and the old layout is still read.
/// - The life-event info keeps a raw FXList pointer that
///   FXParticleSystem::LifeEventModuleInfo::getEventFX caches once a lookup
///   succeeds and that nothing in this group clears,
///   so FX lists must stay at stable addresses for as long as particle
///   templates exist.

/// \fn FXParticleSystem::ParticleSystemTemplate::ParticleSystemTemplate(const AsciiString &name)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_fx_particle_modules
/// \brief Creates a particle-system template with the given name and
///        default settings.
///
/// This is the constructor the FXParticleSystem INI block uses.
/// INI::parseParticleSystemDefinition reads the block's name and asks the
/// particle-system manager for a template of that name
/// (ParticleSystemManager::findTemplate). If none exists the manager creates
/// one. If one exists, the parser destroys it in place, runs this
/// constructor on the same storage, and then parses the block into it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The base settings (FXParticleSystem::ParticleSystemInfo) and the
/// trailing member block are
/// default-constructed, the name is copied, and the cached slave-template
/// pointer is set to null. The trailing block's role is not established.
///
/// \param name The template's name, as written after FXParticleSystem in INI
///             and used for lookups by name.
///
/// \par Modding
/// A second FXParticleSystem block with an existing name replaces the earlier
/// definition entirely: the template is rebuilt from defaults before the new
/// block is parsed, so a field the new block leaves out does not keep its
/// earlier value.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's INI::parseParticleSystemDefinition parses a repeated
///       block over the existing template without resetting it, so there a
///       later block changes only the fields it names.
///
/// \see FXParticleSystem::ParticleSystemTemplate::parse,
///      INI::parseParticleSystemDefinition, ParticleSystemManager::findTemplate

/// \fn FXParticleSystem::ParticleSystemTemplate::ParticleSystemTemplate(const ParticleSystemTemplate &other)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_fx_particle_modules
/// \brief Copies a particle-system template, except for its cached slave
///        template.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Copies the base settings with FXParticleSystem::ParticleSystemInfo's copy
/// constructor, the name, and the trailing member block with that block's
/// own copy function. The cached slave-template pointer starts as null
/// instead of being copied.
///
/// That pointer caches the template named by the SlaveSystem field. BFME 2's
/// slave-system creation code looks that name up with
/// ParticleSystemManager::findTemplate only while the pointer is null and
/// the name is not empty, and stores the result in the pointer. A copy
/// starts with a null pointer, so it repeats the lookup instead of sharing
/// the source's result. Zero Hour's ParticleSystemTemplate keeps the same
/// kind of cache in its createSlaveSystem member function, but creates a
/// slave system on every call once the template is known. BFME 2's creation
/// code creates the slave system only on the call that performs the lookup,
/// and only when that lookup finds a template. Which code copies whole
/// templates is not established here.
///
/// \param other The template to copy.
///
/// \see FXParticleSystem::ParticleSystemTemplate::operator=

/// \fn virtual FXParticleSystem::ParticleSystemTemplate::~ParticleSystemTemplate()
/// \ingroup grp_w3_fx_particle_modules
/// \brief Destroys a particle-system template: the trailing member block,
///        the name, then the base settings.
///
/// When an FXParticleSystem block redefines a template,
/// INI::parseParticleSystemDefinition destroys it in place with a virtual
/// destructor call, without freeing its memory, and then constructs the
/// template again in the same storage. The direct callers of this body
/// include the template's scalar and vector deleting destructors (that
/// virtual call goes through the vector deleting destructor) and a cleanup
/// callback that the compiled code registers with atexit when it first
/// constructs a function-local static template named `--nullsystem`, so
/// that static template is destroyed through this destructor at program
/// exit.
///
/// \see FXParticleSystem::ParticleSystemTemplate::ParticleSystemTemplate

/// \fn FXParticleSystem::ParticleSystemTemplate & FXParticleSystem::ParticleSystemTemplate::operator=(const ParticleSystemTemplate &other)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_fx_particle_modules
/// \brief Assigns one particle-system template to another and clears the
///        cached slave template.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Assigns the base settings with FXParticleSystem::ParticleSystemInfo's
/// assignment operator,
/// then the name and the trailing member block, and sets the cached
/// slave-template pointer to null, matching the copy constructor.
///
/// \param other The template to copy from.
/// \return This template.
///
/// \see FXParticleSystem::ParticleSystemTemplate::ParticleSystemTemplate

/// \fn const FXList * FXParticleSystem::LifeEventModuleInfo::getEventFX()
/// \ingroup grp_w3_fx_particle_modules
/// \brief Returns the FX list named by the module's EventFX field, looking
///        it up on the first call.
///
/// The EventFX field is stored as a plain string while INI is read, so the
/// name is resolved only when this function is called. Its two direct
/// callers are constructors that build an object from a life-event info:
/// each samples the info's EventTime once and stores the FX list this
/// function returns. Which classes those constructors belong to is not established
/// here.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// While the cached pointer is null, the name (or an empty string when no
/// name was set) is passed to FXListStore::findFXList on TheFXListStore and
/// the result is cached. findFXList returns null for None (compared without
/// regard to case) and for a name it does not know; a null result is not
/// remembered, so every later call looks the name up again until it
/// succeeds.
///
/// \return The FX list, or null when the name is empty, None or unknown.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - The EventFX field parser stores the name without looking it up, and
///   the name is resolved only when one of the two constructors above first
///   asks for it. The FX list therefore does not have to exist when the
///   FXParticleSystem block is parsed, only when that lookup runs; a lookup
///   that finds nothing is retried on each later call.
/// - A misspelt EventFX name is not reported here; the effect is simply
///   missing. FX list names are matched through the name-key table, which
///   is case-sensitive.
///
/// \see FXListStore::findFXList, FXParticleSystem::LifeEventModuleInfo::xfer

/// \fn virtual void FXParticleSystem::LifeEventModuleInfo::xfer(Xfer *xfer)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_fx_particle_modules
/// \brief Saves, loads or checksums the life-event data: EventTime and the
///        EventFX name.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Returns at once in light-CRC mode. Otherwise it transfers a version
/// record (version 1), EventTime with xferRandomVariable, and then the EventFX
/// name. The cached FX list pointer is neither transferred nor reset.
/// getEventFX looks the name up only while the cache is still null, so an
/// info whose cache is already set keeps returning that list even if a
/// load changes the name. Copying or assigning an info copies the cached
/// pointer along with the name.
///
/// \param xfer The Xfer doing the save, load or checksum.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The transfer order (version, EventTime, EventFX) is the save format.
///
/// \see xferRandomVariable, FXParticleSystem::LifeEventModuleInfo::getEventFX

/// \fn virtual FXParticleSystem::LifeEventModuleInfo::~LifeEventModuleInfo()
/// \ingroup grp_w3_fx_particle_modules
/// \brief Destroys the life-event data, releasing the EventFX name string.
///
/// Its callers include the info's deleting destructors and the destructors
/// of classes that embed the info. The terrain-collision info's vector
/// deleting destructor and TerrainCollisionModuleTemplate's destructor also
/// call this body, so the retail build uses it as the terrain-collision
/// info's destructor too. The cached FX list belongs to TheFXListStore and
/// is not freed.

/// \fn FXParticleSystem::RenderObjectDrawModuleInfo::RenderObjectDrawModuleInfo()
/// \ingroup grp_w3_fx_particle_modules
/// \brief Sets the defaults of the render-object draw module.
///
/// Its callers include RenderObjectDrawModuleTemplate's constructor, which
/// runs before the module's INI fields are parsed, so these are the values
/// a block leaves unchanged.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// MultiRenderObjects and SinkOnTerrainCollision start false and SinkRate
/// at 0. Each of the three render groups starts with an empty name, a count
/// and a percentage of 0, and the shader W3D_DIFFUSE.
///
/// \see FXParticleSystem::RenderObjectDrawModuleInfo::xfer

/// \fn virtual FXParticleSystem::RenderObjectDrawModuleInfo::~RenderObjectDrawModuleInfo()
/// \ingroup grp_w3_fx_particle_modules
/// \brief Destroys the render-object draw data, releasing the three
///        render-group name strings.
///
/// Its callers include RenderObjectDrawModuleTemplate's destructor, the
/// info's deleting destructors and the destructors of other classes that
/// embed the info.

/// \fn FXParticleSystem::RenderObjectDrawModuleInfo & FXParticleSystem::RenderObjectDrawModuleInfo::operator=(const RenderObjectDrawModuleInfo &that)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_fx_particle_modules
/// \brief Copies every render-object draw setting from another info.
///
/// RenderObjectDrawModuleTemplate's assignment operator uses it for the
/// template's info part. The three group names are assigned with
/// AsciiString's assignment operator; every other member is copied
/// directly.
///
/// \param that The info to copy from.
/// \return This info.

/// \fn virtual void FXParticleSystem::RenderObjectDrawModuleInfo::xfer(Xfer *xfer)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_fx_particle_modules
/// \brief Saves, loads or checksums the render-object draw settings.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Transfers a version record (version 1), then MultiRenderObjects, then
/// each of the three render groups in turn (name, NumObjects, Percent, and
/// the shader as a ParticleShaderType enumeration), and finally
/// SinkOnTerrainCollision and SinkRate. Unlike the life-event and
/// render-object update infos it has no light-CRC early return.
///
/// \param xfer The Xfer doing the save, load or checksum.
///
/// \see FXParticleSystem::RenderObjectDrawModuleInfo::RenderObjectDrawModuleInfo

/// \fn virtual void FXParticleSystem::RenderObjectUpdateModuleInfo::xfer(Xfer *xfer)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_fx_particle_modules
/// \brief Saves, loads or checksums the render-object update settings:
///        twelve size and angle ranges and the rotation mode.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Returns at once in light-CRC mode. Otherwise it transfers a version
/// record (version 1), then the twelve random ranges with
/// xferRandomVariable in this order: StartSizeX, StartSizeY, StartSizeZ,
/// SizeRateX, SizeRateY, SizeRateZ, SizeDampingX, SizeDampingY,
/// SizeDampingZ, AngleZ, AngularRateZ, AngularDamping. Last comes the
/// Rotation mode as a RotationType enumeration.
///
/// \param xfer The Xfer doing the save, load or checksum.
///
/// \see xferRandomVariable

/// \fn virtual void FXParticleSystem::LightningDrawModuleInfo::xfer(Xfer *xfer)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_fx_particle_modules
/// \brief Saves, loads or checksums the lightning draw settings.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Transfers a version record (version 1), the OffsetX, OffsetY and OffsetZ
/// ranges with xferRandomVariable, MultiChance as a real and TileTexture as
/// a bool. There is no light-CRC early return.
///
/// \param xfer The Xfer doing the save, load or checksum.
///
/// \see xferRandomVariable

/// \fn FXParticleSystem::GpuDrawModuleInfo::GpuDrawModuleInfo(const GpuDrawModuleInfo &that)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_fx_particle_modules
/// \brief Copies the GPU draw settings from another info.
///
/// Its callers include GpuDrawModuleTemplate's copy constructor, which uses
/// it for the template's info part. It copies FramesPerRow, TotalFrames, the DetailTexture name and
/// SpeedMultiplier.
///
/// \param that The info to copy.
///
/// \note The GPU draw module has no BFME 1 counterpart; everything said
///       about it comes from the BFME 2 bodies, field table and INI writer.
///       The field names suggest an animated texture sheet, but how the
///       renderer uses them is not covered here.
/// \see FXParticleSystem::GpuDrawModuleInfo::operator=,
///      FXParticleSystem::GpuDrawModuleInfo::DoXfer

/// \fn virtual FXParticleSystem::GpuDrawModuleInfo::~GpuDrawModuleInfo()
/// \ingroup grp_w3_fx_particle_modules
/// \brief Destroys the GPU draw data, releasing the DetailTexture name.
///
/// Its callers include GpuDrawModuleTemplate's destructor, the info's
/// deleting destructors and the destructors of other classes that embed
/// the info.

/// \fn FXParticleSystem::GpuDrawModuleInfo & FXParticleSystem::GpuDrawModuleInfo::operator=(const GpuDrawModuleInfo &that)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_fx_particle_modules
/// \brief Copies the GPU draw settings from another info.
///
/// GpuDrawModuleTemplate's assignment operator uses it for the template's
/// info part. The DetailTexture name is assigned with AsciiString's
/// assignment operator and the three numbers are copied directly.
///
/// \param that The info to copy from.
/// \return This info.

/// \fn virtual void FXParticleSystem::GpuDrawModuleInfo::DoXfer(Xfer &xfer)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_fx_particle_modules
/// \brief Saves, loads or checksums the GPU draw settings.
///
/// It occupies the same Snapshot virtual slot as the other infos' transfer
/// functions; retail's export table spells it DoXfer, as it does
/// FXParticleSystem::ParticleSystemInfo::DoXfer (\ref sub_save_load_crc).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Transfers a version record (version 1), then FramesPerRow, TotalFrames,
/// the DetailTexture name and SpeedMultiplier, in the order the info stores
/// them. There is no light-CRC early return.
///
/// \param xfer The Xfer doing the save, load or checksum.
///
/// \see FXParticleSystem::GpuDrawModuleInfo::GpuDrawModuleInfo

/// \fn virtual Coord3D FXParticleSystem::OutwardEmissionVelocityModule::getVelocity(const Coord3D *pos, EmissionVolumeModuleInterface *vol)
/// \ingroup grp_w3_fx_particle_modules
/// \brief Samples the module's Speed and OtherSpeed ranges and asks an
///        emission volume module for the resulting velocity.
///
/// This module does not choose a direction itself: it samples both speeds
/// and passes them, with \p pos, to \p vol's
/// EmissionVolumeModuleInterface::getVelocity, and returns that function's
/// result. The direction is therefore decided by the volume module.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Speed and OtherSpeed are each sampled once per call with
/// GameClientRandomVariable::getValue, whether or not the volume uses
/// OtherSpeed. Zero Hour: the OUTWARD velocity type was one case of a
/// switch in the particle system's computeParticleVelocity function, which
/// handled each volume shape itself, and only its cylinder and line cases
/// used OtherSpeed. For a cylinder, Speed is radial in the horizontal
/// plane and OtherSpeed vertical; for a sphere or box, Speed points away
/// from the centre; for a line, Speed is horizontal and perpendicular to the
/// line, and OtherSpeed points along the direction perpendicular to both
/// the line and Speed (vertical when the line is horizontal); for a point,
/// Speed is applied in a random
/// direction. Whether each BFME 2 volume keeps these rules is decided by
/// that volume's own getVelocity.
///
/// This function is reached only through a virtual call, and its BFME 2
/// caller is not established here, so which position and which volume
/// module that caller passes are not documented. Zero Hour: the particle's
/// position is computed before its velocity so that the OUTWARD case can
/// use it.
///
/// \param pos A position, passed unchanged to \p vol's getVelocity.
/// \param vol The emission volume module whose getVelocity is called with
///            \p pos and the two sampled speeds.
/// \return The velocity that \p vol's getVelocity returns.
///
/// \par Saving
/// In retail the outward module's vtable points to the same transfer
/// function as the cylindrical module's, because the two modules keep
/// their two ranges in the same place and the build shares identical code.
/// After a version record it saves both ranges (here Speed, then
/// OtherSpeed) with xferRandomVariable.
///
/// \see FXParticleSystem::CylindricalEmissionVelocityModule::xfer

/// \file System/FXParticleSystem/xferRandomVariable.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_fx_particle_modules
/// \brief xferRandomVariable: the save format of a GameClientRandomVariable.

/// \fn Xfer & xferRandomVariable(Xfer &xfer, GameClientRandomVariable &var)
/// \brief Saves, loads or checksums one game-client random range.
///
/// Particle data stores many random ranges, and this is the shared
/// function that transfers them. Its callers include
/// FXParticleSystem::ParticleSystemInfo::DoXfer,
/// FXParticleSystem::OrthoEmissionVelocityModule::xfer,
/// FXParticleSystem::CylindricalEmissionVelocityModule::xfer,
/// FXParticleSystem::DefaultUpdateModuleInfo::xfer and the life-event,
/// render-object update and lightning draw infos in this group.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Transfers a version record (version 1), then the low value and the high
/// value as reals, then the distribution type as an enumeration labelled
/// GameClientRandomVariable::DistributionType. When the Xfer is storing,
/// the three values are taken from the variable first. Otherwise
/// they start as 0, 0 and CONSTANT, and after the transfer
/// GameClientRandomVariable::setRange writes what was read back into the
/// variable.
///
/// \param xfer The Xfer doing the save, load or checksum.
/// \param var  The random range to transfer; written on load.
/// \return xfer, so calls can be chained.
///
/// \note Zero Hour's ParticleSystemInfo transferred each
///       GameClientRandomVariable as raw bytes with one xferUser call. BFME
///       2, like BFME 1, gives the range its own version and three typed
///       fields.
/// \par Remastering
/// This function fixes the save layout of every random range in particle
/// data; a change here changes all of them at once.
///
/// \see GameClientRandomVariable::setRange, GameClientRandomVariable::getValue
